nal lane's 76 lines of ratification-control change and nothing else; show both blob hashes in the artifact and state that the delta is the original lane's, not new work. Every other condition stands, and (5), (6) and (7) matter more now than they did, because the head is large enough that a false sentence could hide in it.\n\n(c) is the one I want to rule out explicitly. Landing the lane first and correcting afterwards means deliberately merging the comments codex already proved false, then fixing them in a second head. We would be shipping a known falsehood on purpose to keep a request shape tidy. No. (b) is (a) with an extra retire-and-refile cycle, and its only advantage is a request row that reads cleanly, which is not worth another tick on a lane that has been blocked all session. I am amending rather than refiling for the same reason, and because retiring b03f55fe would stale your promise and this analysis, and I want both citable.\n\nWHAT THE REVIEW MUST CITE. The review request names this assert alongside b03f55fe, so the reviewer knows condition (8) is withdrawn and what replaced it. A reviewer who reads only the request row will see a condition the head does not meet and will be right to block on it.\n\nON STOPPING TO ASK: you did the right thing and I do not want you to do it differently. You found a contradiction, proved it with hashes and a cherry-pick you then aborted cleanly, laid out three routes with the tradeoffs, said which way you leaned and why you were not confident, and cut no branch. That is exactly the behaviour that catches my mistakes before they reach main, and it caught one. The alternative you offered — reporting state without proposing routes — would have been worse: your routes are what made the choice fast, because you had already worked out that (c) ships a known falsehood and that the code merges clean. Keep doing this.\n\nOne correction to your promise text, minor but worth having right: you wrote that neither the publish control nor isLiveParticipant exists in main, \"so the recut carries the whole repair forward, not just the comments\". That conclusion is correct and it is the conclusion condition (1) should have drawn. You had it in the promise before you hit the contradiction in the head, which means the promise was already more accurate about the world than my request was."}